  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 303 AM CDT Sun Sep 27 2026\n\nScattered showers are continuing to pivot across portions of \nthe Northland early this morning. This activity is tied to a \ncompact shortwave moving across the area, interacting with a \nplume of moisture. The highest rainfall amounts reported so far \nof around a half inch are focused around the Brainerd Lakes \narea. The radar indicates these higher values may extend north \nto western Koochiching county as well, and that there may be \nsome locally higher values out there. These showers should \nsteadily taper off from west to east through the mid to late \nmorning hours as the shortwave pulls away. For the remainder of \ntoday, expect gradually clearing skies as drier air filters into\nthe area. Sunshine will help boost temperatures into the mid \n60s to low 70s, with the warmest temperatures across central and\nnorth-central Minnesota. \n\nMonday and Tuesday will have busier weather as a deep trough \ndigs into the Rockies and high plains. Moisture return ahead of \nthe trough will have a tropical/monsoonal connection- The trough\ntaps into the rich tropical moisture from Pacific Hurricanes \nPolo and Odalys and sweeps it northeast across the plains in our\ndirection. Clouds will increase on Monday, and with the mild \nairmass pushing into the area, we should have a warm day with \nhighs in the mid 60s to mid 70s. The first wave of precipitation\nwith this system pushes into the area Monday afternoon and \nnight, which is focused mainly on the northwestern portions of \nthe forecast area, north-central Minnesota. Additional waves of \nrainfall can be expected Tuesday and Tuesday night, progressing \nto the east to affect the remainder of the forecast area. \nEmbedded thunderstorms are possible Monday night into Tuesday, \nwhich could enhance local rainfall rates. The precipitation \nwill gradually end on Wednesday as the zone of lift and moisture\nshifts to our east. After the warm day on Monday, temperatures \nwill be cooler Tuesday, but then warm back to near to above \nnormal temperatures for Wednesday. \n\nA strong cold front is forecast to sweep southeastward across \nour region Wednesday night and Thursday, bringing a much cooler \nCanadian airmass into the Great Lakes region. Highs on Thursday \nthrough Saturday will fall back into the 50s and low 60s. This \ncooler airmass will also bring the potential for widespread \nfrost or freeze conditions overnight Thursday night into the \nweekend.",
